1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Aaslaug Aasland''' (11 August 1890 – 30 August 1962) was a Norwegian politician for the Labour Party. She was the Norwegian Minister of Social Affairs from 1948 to 1953.<br/>
2 CONT She was born in Sandnes as a daughter of Hans Aasland (1855–1901) and Hanna Marie Nielsen (1857–1957). She took the examen artium in 1916, enrolled at the Royal Frederick University and graduated with the cand.jur. degree in 1922.
2 CONC  She worked for the district stipendiary magistrate in Alta for a short time, and then worked for Norwegian National Women's Council from 1924 to 1931, as a prison inspector from 1931 to 1936 and labour inspector from 1936 to 1945
2 CONC . In 1945 she briefly served as the director of Bredtveit women's prison, which had been a concentration camp during World War II's occupation of Norway by Nazi Germany.<br/>
2 CONT Later in 1945, when Gerhardsen's Second Cabinet assumed office, Aasland became a consultative minister in the Ministry of Social Affairs. She held this post until 1948 when she succeeded Sven Oftedal as Minister of Social Affairs.
2 CONC  She was the first female head of a government ministry (Kirsten Hansteen had preceded Aasland as minister in 1945, but as consultative minister), and also the first female government minister belonging to the Labour Party. She wa
2 CONC s also a member of Oslo city council from 1945 to 1947.<br/>
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aaslaug_Aasland read more]
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aaslaug_Aasland
