1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * Gards- og ættesoga for Hå, bind I, Rimestad, side 17, nr. 60.4, https://www.nb.no/items/URN:NBN:no-nb_digibok_2017012629003?page=19
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Ivar var farmer i N-Dakota, seinere bankmann, gift i Amerika.
2 CONT
2 CONT Immigration: 1893 [1920 census in Storlie, Cavalier Co., ND] 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://hampden.wordpress.com/category/church-history/ '''Hampden (ND) Church History''']:  The oldest of the churches is the ''Zoar Free Lutheran''.  It was organized in 1902 but even before that time, services were held in pion
2 CONC eers’ homes that were scattered out over the prairies.  The first Christmas program was held in the Lars Dahl sod house, which was a very large house for one of that type.  The next year the Christmas festival was held in the Mrs.
2 CONC  Carroll hotel with a large crowd in attendance.  The Christmas offering of $34.75 was presented to the pastor.  It was Pastor J.O. Arvig who served these pioneer people.  He had bee a pastor at Adrian, Minnesota, when he felt the
2 CONC  urge to pioneer with the word of God to the wilds of North Dakota.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1899, although these people were not organized into a regular congregation, the usual ministerial services were conducted.  In 1902 a group of people decided to organize their own congregation, call their own pastor whom it was
2 CONC  decided should be from the Lutheran Free Church.  A constitution was drawn up and the following officers elected: president, Iver Iverson; secretary, Martin Marinson; treasurer, Tobias Bjorland; trustees: Farlo Olson, Lars Dahl a
2 CONC nd Tobias Bjorland; deacons: Mr. and Mrs. Iver Iverson and Dioren Iverson.  There were three people confirmed and one wedding held in 1902.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the fall of 1903 the congregation ventured forth on a building project.  The railroad had not arrived at Hampden at this time so all the church lumber was hauled by team from Edmore.  A plot of land was given by Mr. Stoltz, who
2 CONC  owned the town site.  Carpenters were hired although men of the congregation did much of the work of constructing the church building.  Many town people, although not members of the congregation donated both money and material.  
2 CONC The total bill for the new church including labor, hardware, lumber, insurance and altar furniture, was $2,537.67, although the bill was not paid in full until 1906.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1940 the church was moved to its present location and remodeled.  Colored windows were placed and pews were installed.  Since that year there have been various remodelings done.
