1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Anna Marie Jensen (1854-1896) Immigrant from Farsund, Norway to USA in 1882 (b. 1854, Farsund, Vest-Agder, Norway - d. circa 1895, Chicago, Cook County, Illinois, USA) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Baptism in Farsund: [http://www.arkivverket.no/URN:kb_read?idx_kildeid=870&idx_id=870&uid=ny&idx_side=-32]
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the daughter of Anna Marie Kjarvaldsen (1819-1888) aka '''Anne Maria Gabrielsdatter''' (Lund?); and '''Jens Jacob Hansen''' (1823-1864).
2 CONT
2 CONT Census Farsund 1865: [http://digitalarkivet.no/cgi-win/webcens.exe?slag=visbase&sidenr=9&filnamn=f61003&gardpostnr=181&personpostnr=1372&merk=1372#ovre]
2 CONT
2 CONT Siblings:
2 CONT
2 CONT She had the following siblings: Hans Kristian Jensen (1844-?); Hans Gabriel Jensen (1846-?) who was born on May 12, 1846 and baptised May 21, 1846; Anton Severin Jensen (1849-1906) who was born on December 19, 1849; Juliane Amalie
2 CONC  Jensen (1852-?) who was born on November 20, 1852; Johanne Katrine Jensen (1856-?) who was born on November 14, 1856 and married a Steffen Barca (c1855-before1900); Jens Julius Jensen (1859-c1903) who married Anna Olsen (1860-?);
2 CONC  Andrew Havig Jensen (1861-1930) who married Lena Elaine Olsen (1860-1938); Johan K. Jensen (1863-?); Henry Jensen; Kristina Jensen; and Tillie Jensen. Other children may exist but have to be confirmed with a death certificate.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage:
2 CONT
2 CONT She married Peter Olsen (1844-1892) and they both may have died before the year 1900. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Together they had the following children: Jennie Olsen (1881-1942) who was born in May of 1881 and married Harry Henning and moved to Hinsdale, Illinois, and died on October 12, 1942; Osborne Theomun Olsen (1883-1971) aka Asbjorn 
2 CONC T. Olsen, aka Ossie Olsen, owner of Osborne Art Studios in Chicago who married Augusta Schmidt (1883-1974) aka Gussie Schmidt; Perry Maranius Olsen (1885-1972) who moved to Oakland, California; and Harriet Olsen (1889-?) who was b
2 CONC orn in March of 1889.  
