1 NAME Elizabeth Ann /Brierley/
2 GIVN Elizabeth Ann
2 SURN Brierley
2 _MARNM Odland
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1923
2 ADDR
3 CITY New Britain
3 STAE Connecticut
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 23 APR 1998
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Elisabeth Brierley Odland grew so many zucchinis at her Whidbey Island vacation home that she left extras along the road for passers-by.
2 CONT
2 CONT She had a similar attitude with the flowers she grew at home in Seattle and with recipes, books and household goods: All were meant for sharing or recycling.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her last triumph was the Northwest Literacy Foundation's "Fill the Shelves" campaign, which put 50,000 new and used books in school libraries. She created the plan after reading about Seattle School District Superintendent John St
2 CONC anford's unsuccessful bid to launch a book levy.
2 CONT
2 CONT "She was something else," said her son John Odland of Seattle. "And such an unselfish and nonspotlight-seeking person. She quietly did a lot of things and wanted no credit."
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Odland died of cancer Thursday (April 23). She was 74.
2 CONT
2 CONT The woman who friends call "Bri" - an abbreviation of her maiden name - also chaired the Lakeside School Mothers' Club Cookbook Committee, which published the locally best-selling Lakeside Cookbook in 1974. She headed the Lakeside
2 CONC  Rummage Sale, a semiannual fund-raiser.
2 CONT
2 CONT And she rarely missed a fund-raising activity for the Alma Stewart Ballinger Children's Hospital Guild, of which she was a 44-year member.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Compiling the cookbook, she tried many of the recipes out on us," said her son. "Everything they put in was good, although she did reject some. She got a great lemon bars recipe that our whole family uses."
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in New Britain, Conn., Mrs. Odland married George Odland in 1945 and lived in Boston while he finished his medical training.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1954 they moved to Seattle, living in a rental cottage before acquiring the Washington Park house they called home the next 41 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although she devoted herself to family and community, she also enjoyed gardening and tennis.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Bri was a quiet, gentle, giving person," said her friend Betsy Minor. "The bottom line was, she was completely devoted to her husband and her family. Everything she did, she did perfectly."
2 CONT
2 CONT "She was a true New England lady, generous but very private," said her friend Virginia Kitchell. "She wouldn't let you do anything for her . . . but her friends loved her dearly."
2 CONT
2 CONT Other survivors include sons Hank Odland of Whidbey Island and Peter Odland of Seattle; brothers George Brierley and Vernon Brierley Jr., Kensington, Conn., and nine grandchildren. Her husband died last year. Services are at 1 p.m
2 CONC . Friday at Epiphany Parish, 38th Avenue and East Denny Way, Seattle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Remembrances may go to the Elisabeth B. Odland Fund of the Northwest Literacy Foundation, P.O. Box 15970, Seattle, WA 98115.
2 CONT
2 CONT 29 April 1998 Seattle Times.
