1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Donald Hamel Venables (1922-2014) passed away peacefully, surrounded by his family on Wednesday, January 8th 2014. Don was born in Yakima Washington and moved to Seattle with the family where he graduated from Lake
2 CONC side High School in 1941. World War II would interrupt his studies at the University of Washington. He joined the Army and served in combat during the latter part of the war in Europe with the 103rd Infantry Division. After the wa
2 CONC r, he returned to graduate from the UW with a degree in business and finance, followed by a long career with Seattle First National Bank. After leaving the bank he remained in the finance and administration industry working for co
2 CONC mpanies such as GR Construction, Black Angus Restaurants, Cascade Savings Mortgage Company and Glacier Real Estate Funding. He was an active member and leader of numerous civic and social organizations such as the Seattle Girls Sc
2 CONC outs Totem Council and 4H, where he served as president. Don will be remembered for his warm personality, quick wit, and love of family. He was a beloved father, grandfather, husband, friend and uncle. He will be greatly missed. D
2 CONC on was preceded in death by his wife Patricia Odland and his son Jim. He is survived by his son, Bill Venables and his wife Heidi Burmeister of Bainbridge Island, daughter Katie Waldrop of Everett, and grandchildren Frannie and Ja
2 CONC mes Waldrop.
2 CONT
2 CONT A memorial service to celebrate his life will be held at Epiphany Parish of Seattle at 4:30 p.m. Friday, January 24th with a reception following. In Lieu of flowers, memorial remembrances can be made to the Bethany Northwest Found
2 CONC ation, P.O Box 5128, Everett, Wa, 98206.
2 CONT To plant trees in memory, please visit the Sympathy Store.
2 CONT
2 CONT Published by The Seattle Times from Jan. 18 to Jan. 19, 2014.
